  "lens": {
   "mechanics": "When spending financed by money and credit grows faster than the goods and services sold, prices rise. Gold pays no interest, so higher bond yields after inflation increase the cost of holding it.",
   "data": "Three forces matter. October consumer prices rose <a href=\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/cpi.nr0.htm\">0.4% on the month</a>, keeping pressure on policy. The 10-year real yield climbed to <a href=\"https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?type=daily_treasury_real_yield_curve&amp;field_tdr_date_value_month=202611\">2.78% on 10 Nov</a>, increasing the buying power offered by bonds. Breakeven inflation eased to 2.20%, so the rise in nominal yields did not come from higher 10-year inflation compensation. In a debt-monetisation episode real returns can fall. Here they rose, which helps explain why gold fell on an inflation surprise.",
   "unsustainable": "Persistent debt growth above revenue growth squeezes future spending. Spending cuts, restructuring, transfers and monetary expansion can each shift the cost of adjustment.",
